1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,643
Hi there! I'm Spud.

2
00:00:01,643 --> 00:00:05,415
We're Group 14, Team Sweet
Potatoes, and this is HearHere:

3
00:00:05,415 --> 00:00:10,357
a street post that helps international
residents understand and answer local

4
00:00:10,357 --> 00:00:11,748
council decisions.

5
00:00:11,748 --> 00:00:15,984
Spud stands for Street Post for
Understanding Decisions. And yes,

6
00:00:15,984 --> 00:00:17,964
it's also slang for potato!

7
00:00:17,964 --> 00:00:19,574
HearHere. Your street, your say.

8
00:00:20,574 --> 00:00:24,767
Melbourne's councils make decisions
about parks, housing and transport.

9
00:00:24,767 --> 00:00:29,023
Our fieldwork with eight international
residents found the barrier isn't a lack

10
00:00:29,023 --> 00:00:29,890
of interest.

11
00:00:29,890 --> 00:00:32,334
People can't find relevant information.

12
00:00:32,334 --> 00:00:35,914
Translated policies still use
terms they don't understand.

13
00:00:35,914 --> 00:00:37,993
And they don't know where
their feedback goes.

14
00:00:37,993 --> 00:00:41,200
Meanwhile, council news
arrives by mail and flyers,

15
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:43,590
but newer residents live on Instagram.

16
00:00:44,290 --> 00:00:45,394
Meet Jason.

17
00:00:45,394 --> 00:00:49,640
He's lived in Melbourne for years,
but English isn't his first language.

18
00:00:49,640 --> 00:00:54,153
He's unhappy about a new park rule for
dogs, yet he doesn't know who to tell,

19
00:00:54,153 --> 00:00:55,443
or which words to use.

20
00:00:56,143 --> 00:00:59,558
On his grocery walk, Jason
passes a HearHere post.

21
00:00:59,558 --> 00:01:02,987
Its pillar shows verified
council and community notices,

22
00:01:02,987 --> 00:01:06,023
so he finds things he'd
never see in the mail.

23
00:01:06,023 --> 00:01:07,951
Hi! Got something to say? I'm all ears.

24
00:01:07,951 --> 00:01:11,496
The screen under Spud lists the
issues closest to this spot,

25
00:01:11,496 --> 00:01:13,623
ranked by distance and closing date.

26
00:01:14,323 --> 00:01:16,110
Jason taps the park issue.

27
00:01:16,110 --> 00:01:19,142
A plain-language summary
appears in his own language,

28
00:01:19,142 --> 00:01:21,135
with difficult terms explained.

29
00:01:21,135 --> 00:01:24,191
Now he simply talks to
Spud, in his own language.

30
00:01:24,191 --> 00:01:27,887
The new park rule makes walking my dog
really inconvenient. I hope it can be

31
00:01:27,887 --> 00:01:28,739
reconsidered.

32
00:01:28,739 --> 00:01:33,443
Spud turns that into a structured
comment, original Chinese plus English,

33
00:01:33,443 --> 00:01:38,818
names the responsible department, and
lets Jason review and edit before sending.

34
00:01:38,818 --> 00:01:40,154
No audio is stored.

35
00:01:41,054 --> 00:01:43,664
Jason taps his phone on the NFC pad.

36
00:01:43,664 --> 00:01:48,030
The issue and his comment move to the
HearHere app, and the post keeps nothing.

37
00:01:48,030 --> 00:01:50,803
Tracking begins only
because Jason chose it.

38
00:01:50,803 --> 00:01:53,546
The app shows each stage:
Received, In review,

39
00:01:53,546 --> 00:01:57,284
then an outcome with a case
number, or reasons if declined.

40
00:01:57,284 --> 00:01:59,320
Notifications keep him updated.

41
00:02:00,020 --> 00:02:01,386
Not sure who to ask?

42
00:02:01,386 --> 00:02:05,736
The Q&A assistant answers from
official documents, cites its sources,

43
00:02:05,736 --> 00:02:09,172
and hands over to a named
contact when it's unsure.

44
00:02:09,172 --> 00:02:13,462
And one tap shares a card on Instagram,
so Jason's neighbours can join in.

45
00:02:14,162 --> 00:02:18,174
For Jason, that means finding
relevant issues on his everyday route,

46
00:02:18,174 --> 00:02:22,599
understanding them in his own language,
knowing which department hears him,

47
00:02:22,599 --> 00:02:24,530
and seeing what happens next.

48
00:02:24,530 --> 00:02:25,997
All without attending a meeting.

49
00:02:26,697 --> 00:02:28,100
HearHere has limits.

50
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:31,815
Speaking in public may feel
exposing, AI can mistranslate,

51
00:02:31,815 --> 00:02:35,045
and our fieldwork didn't
include council staff.

52
00:02:35,045 --> 00:02:39,405
Next, we'll test a low-fidelity prototype
with residents across languages,

53
00:02:39,405 --> 00:02:42,763
and check whether they trust
and correct the AI's output.

54
00:02:43,463 --> 00:02:47,296
HearHere, with Spud: the Street
Post for Understanding Decisions.

55
00:02:47,296 --> 00:02:48,388
Speak here. Be heard!
